#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088817} :activity/id #uuid "115bfa9d-34ae-51ca-aa04-b0f23cf266a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569 :topic/id #uuid "aa9fc29e-db88-57a6-bfa7-3e6a009ad93e" :topic/math-academy-id 877} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088859 :tutorial/content "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." :tutorial/id #uuid "1066a4d7-d790-5316-9925-1ac0e928ce01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14550 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2aeafed5-8ce5-5873-95fe-baefd4b2fe7b" :step/math-academy-id 597598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088860 :tutorial/content "Given a first-order linear ODE, we can find an integrating factor and use it to solve the equation using the following procedure.\n\n- **Step 1**: Write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\,.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Multiply both sides of the equation found in step 1 by $I(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + I(x)P(x)y = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 4**: Rewrite the left side as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr)\n$$\n\nIf steps 1 through 3 are done correctly, then we can always do this, and we can check that it's correct by differentiating. Our new equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr) = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 5**: Integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$ and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yI(x) & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{I(x)}(\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might be wondering where the formula for $I(x)$ comes from; we'll discuss this at the end of the lesson. First, let's apply this method to a concrete example in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "a6a381ed-e9ab-5deb-9d74-30900610edf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14551 :tutorial/title "The Integrating Factor Method"} :step/id #uuid "552b03ae-2839-592c-a5fa-667890639337" :step/math-academy-id 597599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087452 :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348295}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} :step/id #uuid "0c98b113-16b4-5f8c-8b8c-a5ffbbbd075d" :step/math-academy-id 597600 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391731}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} :step/id #uuid "6c426f6e-80e3-59e6-8472-9394bf844946" :step/math-academy-id 597603 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087454 :question/id #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" :question/math-academy-id "e-21743"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048245}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397296}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"} :step/id #uuid "8f99a521-6d0e-5370-b62d-331c98f13937" :step/math-academy-id 597607 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088861 :tutorial/content "We will now derive the formula for the integrating factor. \n\nConsider the first-order linear equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the left-hand side as the derivative of a product.\n\nTo do that, we multiply both sides of the equation by some function $I(x)$ that we wish to determine.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I(x)P(x)}y} = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe want the left-hand side to match the derivative of $y\\cdot I(x)$. But by the product rule,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr)\n=\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I'(x)}y}.\n$$\n\nSo, if we choose $I(x)$ so that\n\n$$\nI'(x) = I(x)P(x),\n$$\n\nthen the left-hand side becomes $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr).$\n\nDividing both sides of $I'(x) = I(x)P(x)$ by $I(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{I'(x)}{I(x)} = P(x).\n$$\n\nNext, integrating both sides with respect to $x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{I}^{'}(x)}{I(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}(I(x)))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(I(x)) & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{C}\\cdot {e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, because multiplying $I(x)$ by a nonzero constant does not change the method, we can choose $e^C = 1$ and define an integrating factor as\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a7f8aa2e-e022-57eb-be82-5f539a4512f6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14640 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Integrating Factor"} :step/id #uuid "ad072f0e-cc4b-5f68-b499-63509c901c8a" :step/math-academy-id 597611}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087942}]}
